`),sampleAnswer:["The Brønsted–Lowry model defines acids as **proton donors** and bases as **proton acceptors**. It is an improvement on the Arrhenius theory as it explains a broader range of acid–base behaviour.","","**Advantage:** The Brønsted–Lowry model classifies substances as acids or bases that Arrhenius theory cannot. For example, ammonia is a Brønsted–Lowry base even in reactions without water:","","$$\\mathrm{NH_3(g) + HCl(g) \\rightarrow NH_4Cl(s)}$$","","Here $\\mathrm{HCl}$ donates a proton to $\\mathrm{NH_3}$. Ammonia does not produce $\\mathrm{OH^-}$, so it is not an Arrhenius base, yet the Brønsted–Lowry model correctly classifies it as a base.","","Another example is the dissociation of a weak acid in water:","","$$\\mathrm{CH_3COOH(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons CH_3COO^-(aq) + H_3O^+(aq)}$$","","Here water acts as a Brønsted–Lowry base (proton acceptor), which Arrhenius theory does not recognise.","","**Limitation:** The Brønsted–Lowry model does not explain the acidity of acidic oxides such as $\\mathrm{SO_3}$ reacting with basic oxides such as $\\mathrm{CaO}$:","","$$\\mathrm{CaO(s) + SO_3(g) \\rightarrow CaSO_4(s)}$$","","There is no proton transfer in this reaction, so Brønsted–Lowry theory cannot classify it as an acid–base reaction.","","Overall, the Brønsted–Lowry model is **useful** for explaining many acid–base reactions in aqueous and non-aqueous systems, but has limitations when no proton transfer occurs."].join(`
