`),sampleAnswer:["**Boiling points increase with increasing chain length** for both series.","Longer carbon chains have more electrons and greater surface area, leading to stronger **dispersion forces** (London/van der Waals forces). More energy is required to separate molecules with stronger dispersion forces, so boiling point increases.","","**Alcohols have higher boiling points than the analogous thiols.**","Alcohols can form **hydrogen bonds** ($\\mathrm{O\\!-\\!H\\!\\cdots\\!O}$) because oxygen is highly electronegative and the O–H bond is strongly polarised. Hydrogen bonds are significantly stronger than the dispersion forces between thiol molecules (S–H bonds are weaker hydrogen bond donors/acceptors due to the lower electronegativity of sulfur). More energy is required to separate alcohol molecules, giving higher boiling points.","","**The difference between analogous alcohols and thiols decreases as chain length increases.**","As chain length increases, dispersion forces grow in magnitude for both series. The contribution of hydrogen bonding in alcohols becomes relatively smaller compared to the growing dispersion forces. Therefore, the boiling point difference between alcohols and thiols decreases."].join(`
