`),sampleAnswer:["**Infrared spectrum:**","The broad absorption at $3300\\text{–}3400\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is due to the N–H stretching of the **amino ($\\mathrm{-NH_2}$) groups**, confirming the presence of primary amines at both ends of the molecule.","","**Mass spectrum:**","The fragment at $m/z = 30$ corresponds to $\\mathrm{CH_2NH_2^+}$ ($12 + 4 \\times 1 + 14 = 30\\ \\mathrm{g\\,mol^{-1}}$), which arises from cleavage of the $\\mathrm{C_1\\text{–}C_2}$ bond adjacent to the terminal amino group.","","**Carbon-13 NMR:**","Pentane-1,5-diamine has 5 carbon atoms, but the molecule is **symmetric**. Carbons 1 and 5 are in identical environments; carbons 2 and 4 are equivalent; carbon 3 is unique. This gives only **three** $\\mathrm{^{13}C}$ NMR signals.","The peak at $\\sim 42\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ corresponds to C1 and C5 ($\\mathrm{-CH_2-NH_2}$, shifted downfield by the $\\mathrm{N}$ atom). Peaks at $\\sim 33\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ and $\\sim 24\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ correspond to C2/C4 and C3 respectively (aliphatic $\\mathrm{-CH_2-}$ carbons).","","**Proton NMR:**","Protons on C3 (central $\\mathrm{CH_2}$) are flanked by 4 equivalent H atoms on C2 and C4, producing a **quintet** signal.","The overlapping quintets at $\\sim 1.4\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ (highlighted, 6H) result from the near-coincidence of the C3 protons (2H, quintet) and C2/C4 protons (4H, quintet), giving an apparent 6H signal.","The triplet at $\\sim 2.6\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ (4H) arises from $\\mathrm{-CH_2-NH_2}$ (C1 and C5) protons, each adjacent to 2H on C2/C4."].join(`
`),marks:7})),Ple=D("chemistry/2021/q30",()=>({question:["A student was trying to identify the ions present in a dilute aqueous solution.","","The solution contained ions of barium, calcium or magnesium, and ions of hydroxide or acetate.","","The student performed the following tests and recorded their observations. A fresh sample of the solution was used for each test.","","- When aqueous sodium chloride was added, **no visible reaction** was observed.","- When aqueous silver nitrate was added, a **brown precipitate** was produced. The precipitate dissolved when dilute hydrochloric acid was added.","- When concentrated aqueous sodium sulfate was added, a **white precipitate** was produced.","","Evaluate this procedure as a method of identifying the ions."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Test 1 — Adding aqueous sodium chloride:**","This test is not useful. Sodium does not precipitate with hydroxide or acetate under these conditions, and chloride does not precipitate with barium, calcium, or magnesium. No conclusions can be drawn.","","**Test 2 — Adding aqueous silver nitrate:**","This test correctly identifies the anion. Silver acetate is soluble, but **silver hydroxide** is an insoluble, brown precipitate that dissolves when excess HCl is added. This indicates the anion is **hydroxide ($\\mathrm{OH^-}$)**.","Magnesium hydroxide ($\\mathrm{Mg(OH)_2}$) is insoluble, so magnesium is eliminated as the cation.","","**Test 3 — Adding concentrated sodium sulfate:**","Both $\\mathrm{BaSO_4}$ and $\\mathrm{CaSO_4}$ are insoluble, so a white precipitate would form with **either barium or calcium**. This test cannot distinguish between them.","","**Conclusion:**","The procedure successfully identifies the anion as $\\mathrm{OH^-}$ and eliminates magnesium, but it **cannot distinguish between barium and calcium**. A flame test would be needed to identify the cation.","Therefore, this is **not an adequate** procedure for completely identifying both ions."].join(`
