`),sampleAnswer:["The net ionic equation for reactions 1 and 2 is:","$$\\mathrm{H^+(aq) + OH^-(aq) \\rightarrow H_2O(l)}$$","","$\\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\mathrm{HNO_3}$ are both **strong acids** and are **completely ionised** in solution.","The neutralisation in both cases is simply $\\mathrm{H^+}$ reacting with $\\mathrm{OH^-}$, so both reactions have the **same net ionic equation** and therefore the **same molar enthalpy** of $-57.6\\ \\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}$.","","$\\mathrm{HCN}$ is a **weak acid** and is **only partially ionised** in solution:","$$\\mathrm{HCN(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons H_3O^+(aq) + CN^-(aq)} \\quad (\\text{endothermic, } \\Delta H > 0)$$","","As $\\mathrm{H^+}$ is consumed during neutralisation, more $\\mathrm{HCN}$ ionises (equilibrium shifts right). This ionisation step is **endothermic**, consuming some of the heat released by the $\\mathrm{H^+ + OH^-}$ reaction.","The overall $\\Delta H$ for reaction 3 is therefore **less negative** (less exothermic) than for reactions 1 and 2."].join(`
