`),marks:2},{question:"Explain the limitations of using income as a single measure of quality of life.",sampleAnswer:["Income measures the monetary rewards earned by residents, but quality of life is broader than income alone.","A high income does not show how evenly income is distributed, whether people enjoy good health and education, or whether they experience environmental damage and work-life balance issues.","As a result, income should be considered alongside other indicators such as life expectancy, literacy and broader wellbeing measures."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:"Discuss the impact of world economic development on environmental sustainability.",sampleAnswer:["World economic development generally increases incomes, trade and production, which can place greater pressure on environmental resources.","Higher output and transport increase energy use, carbon emissions and the depletion of non-renewable resources. Industrialisation and land clearing can also damage ecosystems and reduce long-term environmental sustainability.","However, development can also improve access to cleaner technology and encourage global cooperation on environmental problems. International agreements, renewable energy investment and better environmental management can reduce some of the damage caused by growth.","Overall, world economic development can harm environmental sustainability when production expands without restraint, but it can also support better environmental outcomes when economies use technology and policy to manage that growth."].join(`
