`),m2e=D("economics/2025/q26",()=>({question:["Explain how the dimensions and trends of income inequality affect the Australian economy. In your answer, refer to the information provided.","","Inequality is the uneven distribution of economic resources between individuals or different groups within a society. We care about inequality because income and wealth can be significant contributors to an individual's wellbeing and because inequality can be detrimental to the ongoing advancement of our economy and society.","","Median full-time weekly earnings by age and gender, 2024","",u2e].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["Income inequality affects the Australian economy by changing spending patterns, access to opportunity and long-term growth. The information provided shows that inequality has dimensions linked to both age and gender, with female earnings below male earnings in most age groups and earnings also varying over the life cycle.","These income gaps can reduce access to quality housing, health care, education and technology for lower-income groups. Over time this weakens human capital formation and labour productivity, limiting the economy's productive potential. Lower-income households also tend to spend a larger share of their income on necessities, so inequality can restrain discretionary consumption and reduce aggregate demand growth.","There are also broader social and economic effects. Persistent inequality can reduce social cohesion, worsen health outcomes and make it harder for households to manage rising living costs. If people face barriers to participation or advancement, the economy may fail to use labour efficiently and lose output that could otherwise be produced.","At the same time, some income differences can create incentives to work, save and invest. However, when inequality becomes too wide or entrenched, the negative effects on wellbeing, opportunity and economic participation usually outweigh those benefits. This is why policies such as progressive taxation, transfer payments and improved access to services matter for both equity and economic performance in Australia."].join(`
`),marks:20})),p2e=D("economics/2025/q27",()=>({question:"Analyse the conflicts for government that may arise in pursuing Australia's economic objectives. In your response, refer to THREE separate objectives.",sampleAnswer:["Governments pursue a range of economic objectives, including economic growth, full employment, price stability, external stability, environmental sustainability and a more equitable distribution of income. These objectives are important, but they can conflict with each other, making policy decisions difficult.","One major conflict is between economic growth and price stability. Expansionary fiscal or monetary policy can increase aggregate demand, lift output and reduce unemployment, but if demand grows too strongly it can also increase inflation. This means policies that support growth and employment in the short run may make it harder to keep inflation within target.","A second conflict can arise between full employment and external stability. Strong domestic demand can reduce unemployment, but it may also increase imports and worsen the current account balance. Similarly, policies that slow demand to improve external stability may weaken growth and employment.","A third conflict exists between economic growth and environmental sustainability. Faster growth often increases production, transport and energy use, which can worsen pollution and resource depletion. Policies designed to protect the environment, such as stricter regulation or carbon pricing, may raise costs for firms in the short run and slow growth in some industries.","Governments therefore need to balance competing objectives rather than maximise one objective in isolation. The policy mix chosen will depend on current economic conditions and the relative importance of each objective at the time."].join(`
`),marks:20})),f2e=D("economics/2025/q28",()=>({question:"Analyse how different methods of protection affect firms and government in Australia.",sampleAnswer:["Protection refers to government policies that shelter domestic firms from foreign competition. Methods include tariffs, quotas, subsidies, local content rules and export incentives. These policies affect both firms and government in several ways.","For firms, protection can increase domestic market share and provide time to expand output, protect employment and build scale. Tariffs and quotas make imported goods more expensive or less available, so domestic firms can sell more easily in the local market. Subsidies can reduce production costs and improve competitiveness.","However, protection can also reduce pressure to become efficient. When firms face less foreign competition, they may have weaker incentives to cut costs, innovate or improve quality. Tariffs on imported inputs can also raise production costs for local firms that rely on overseas components.","For government, some protectionist policies provide revenue, especially tariffs. Protection can also help preserve employment in politically sensitive or strategic industries. But subsidies place pressure on the budget, and protectionist measures may create trade disputes, retaliation from trading partners and higher prices for consumers.","Overall, protection can support selected firms in the short term, but if it is maintained for too long it can reduce efficiency and create costs for both government and the broader economy."].join(`
