`),sampleAnswer:["This statement is justified by numerous landmark experiments and observations in","modern physics.","","**Particle–field interactions:**","","Thomson's cathode-ray tube experiment demonstrated that cathode rays were deflected","by electric and magnetic fields. By measuring the deflection, Thomson determined the","$q/m$ ratio of the electron, establishing it as a subatomic particle with definite properties.","","Millikan's oil-drop experiment measured the interaction of charged droplets with an","electric field, allowing him to determine the fundamental charge of the electron ($e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C).","","Observations of the photoelectric effect showed that electromagnetic radiation","(a field) interacts with electrons in discrete quanta. Einstein's explanation revealed","the particle (photon) nature of light, demonstrating that $E = hf$.","","**Particle–particle interactions:**","","Chadwick's experiment bombarded beryllium with alpha particles. The resulting neutral","radiation was shown to knock protons out of paraffin wax. By conservation of momentum,","Chadwick identified the neutron, a previously unknown subatomic particle.","","The Geiger–Marsden experiment fired alpha particles at gold foil. The occasional","large-angle scattering revealed that most of the mass of an atom is concentrated in a","tiny, positively charged nucleus, fundamentally changing the model of the atom.","","Particle accelerator experiments at high energies caused deep inelastic scattering of","electrons off protons, revealing internal structure and leading to the discovery of quarks.","This underpins the Standard Model of particle physics.","","**Conclusion:**","","Each of these experiments used either field–particle or particle–particle interactions","to reveal new properties of matter, justifying the statement that such interactions have","greatly increased our understanding of the physical world."].join(`
