`)},{id:"four-subject-areas",question:"Besides 10 Board Developed units and English, what else does UAC require for ATAR eligibility in NSW?",choices:["At least 4 subject areas","At least 2 science subjects","At least 4 units of mathematics","At least 1 VET course"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["UAC requires at least 4 subject areas for a standard ATAR pattern.","","This is why several extension courses in the same field do not necessarily give","you enough subject breadth for ATAR eligibility."].join(`
`)},{id:"board-developed-course",question:"What is a Board Developed course in the context of ATAR rules?",choices:["A course with a syllabus developed by NESA","Any course your school decides to offer","A university bridging course","A course that only counts in Year 11"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["A Board Developed course has a syllabus developed by NESA.","","UAC says ATAR courses are NESA-developed courses with formal examinations and","graded assessments."].join(`
`)},{id:"best-10-pattern-first",question:"What is the best way to think about UAC using your best 10 units?",choices:["Any 10 units can count, even if the pattern misses subject-area rules","UAC first needs a valid ATAR pattern, then uses the best eligible 10 units","Only your trial-exam units can be dropped","English can be dropped if your other units are stronger"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["UAC first needs a valid ATAR pattern, then uses the best eligible 10 units.","","The pattern still has to satisfy rules such as English and the 4 subject area","requirement before the best-10 calculation matters."].join(`
`)},{id:"hsc-complete-but-no-atar",question:"A student finishes Year 12 with 10 units but only 3 subject areas. Which statement is most accurate?",choices:["They automatically receive an ATAR because 10 units is enough on its own","They may complete the HSC, but still miss ATAR eligibility","They fail the HSC automatically because UAC rejected the pattern","They can ignore the problem because UAC will invent a fourth subject area"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["A student may complete the HSC, but still miss ATAR eligibility if the UAC pattern","is wrong.","","The HSC and the ATAR are related, but they are not the same rule set."].join(`
`)},{id:"final-hsc-mark",question:"Your final HSC mark in a subject is made up of which two equal parts?",choices:["25% classwork and 75% HSC exam","50% moderated school assessment and 50% HSC exam mark","50% trial exam and 50% half-yearly exam","100% final exam mark"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The final HSC mark is made up of 50% moderated school assessment and 50% HSC exam mark.","","The school component is moderated, so it is not just the raw marks your teacher","recorded during the year."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section V - Subject Selection Strategy",description:"How to choose subjects sensibly based on ability, interest, prerequisites, workload, and subject-area rules.",questions:[{id:"subject-selection-balance",question:"Which combination best reflects a strong HSC subject selection strategy?",choices:["Choose only subjects that sound prestigious","Choose subjects based only on scaling tables","Balance ability, enjoyment, and future requirements","Copy the same subjects as your friends"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["A strong subject selection strategy balances ability, enjoyment, and future course","requirements.","","Most students do better in subjects they can handle well and stay motivated in,","rather than chasing prestige or scaling alone."].join(`
`)},{id:"subject-area-definition",question:"In UAC rules, what is a subject area?",choices:["A broader category that can contain multiple related courses","A single assessment task inside a course","A rank band used after the HSC exams","A university faculty such as Science or Law"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["A subject area is a broader category that can contain multiple related courses.","","For example, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, and Mathematics","Extension 2 are different courses in the same Mathematics subject area."].join(`
`)},{id:"course-vs-subject-area",question:"Which statement best shows the difference between a course and a subject area?",choices:["Mathematics Extension 1 is a course; Mathematics is a subject area","Mathematics is a course; Mathematics Extension 1 is a subject area","A course and a subject area mean exactly the same thing","A subject area is just a single assessment task inside a course"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Mathematics Extension 1 is a course, while Mathematics is the broader subject area.","","Several related courses can sit inside one subject area for ATAR rules."].join(`
`)},{id:"best-10-units",question:"Why do some students choose to study 11 units instead of exactly 10?",choices:["Because UAC requires 11 units for every course","Because one weaker unit can act as insurance and be dropped","Because 11 units always gives a higher ATAR automatically","Because English no longer counts if you do 11 units"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Some students study 11 units so one weaker unit can act as insurance and be dropped","from the best 10 units used for the ATAR.","","This only helps if the extra workload is still manageable."].join(`
`)},{id:"science-extension-subject-area",question:"Which statement about Science Extension is correct for ATAR subject areas?",choices:["It creates a brand-new extra subject area by itself","It does not count as an additional subject area","It automatically replaces Biology in your pattern","It is compulsory if you study two science courses"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Science Extension does not count as an additional subject area.","","You can study more than one science course, but Science Extension does not create","extra subject-area breadth on its own."].join(`
`)},{id:"valid-subject-area-pattern",question:"Which pattern is most likely to satisfy the 4 subject area rule for ATAR eligibility?",choices:["English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Mathematics Extension 2, Biology","English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Modern History, Studies of Religion II","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Mathematics Extension 2, Visual Arts","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Mathematics Extension 2, Physics"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Modern History, and Studies of","Religion II gives a full 10-unit pattern with enough subject-area breadth.","","The other options are too concentrated in English and Mathematics, so they do not","have enough subject-area breadth."].join(`
`)},{id:"extension-maths-choice",question:"When is Mathematics Extension 1 usually a sensible choice?",choices:["When you are already struggling heavily in Advanced Maths","When you dislike maths but want the scaling only","When you are strong in maths and genuinely enjoy problem-solving","When you want to avoid doing homework in other subjects"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Mathematics Extension 1 is usually a sensible choice when you are strong in maths","and genuinely enjoy problem-solving.","","It should not be chosen just for scaling if it will drag down your overall","performance."].join(`
`)},{id:"prerequisite-check",question:"Before choosing Year 11 and Year 12 subjects, what should you check if you already have likely university courses in mind?",choices:["Whether the course has prerequisites or assumed knowledge","Whether your friends are applying too","Whether the university campus has free parking","Whether the course is easy to pronounce"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["You should check whether the course has prerequisites or assumed knowledge.","","That helps you avoid locking yourself out of a pathway by dropping a subject too","early."].join(`
`)},{id:"acceleration-purpose",question:"What is one common reason students accelerate an HSC subject?",choices:["To make room in Year 12 or reduce later workload","To remove the English requirement for the ATAR","To avoid sitting any HSC exams","To turn a Board Endorsed course into a university prerequisite"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["A common reason for acceleration is to make room in Year 12 or reduce later","workload.","","For the right student, acceleration can create timetable space and reduce pressure in","the main HSC year."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section VI - Moderation, Scaling and Common Confusions",description:"Common misunderstandings about moderation, scaling, and how students interpret results and rules.",questions:[{id:"moderation-rank",question:"During moderation, what is preserved within your school?",choices:["Your exact raw assessment marks","Your school rank order","Your trial paper mark only","Your attendance percentage"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Your rank order within the school is what is preserved during moderation.","","The raw assessment marks can change after moderation, but the order of students in","that subject at the school is kept."].join(`
`)},{id:"scaling-myth",question:"Which statement about subject scaling is correct?",choices:["Choosing a high-scaling subject automatically gives you a high ATAR","Scaling means your school rank no longer matters","Scaling changes marks, but strong performance still matters most","Scaling only applies to English subjects"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Scaling changes marks, but strong performance still matters most.","","A difficult or high-scaling subject does not guarantee a better ATAR. In general,","doing well in a subject suits you better than doing poorly in one just because it","is known for stronger scaling."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section VII - Results and Post-ATAR Actions",description:"What students should understand about offers, preferences, and next steps after results are released.",questions:[{id:"uac-preferences-ranking",question:"How does UAC use your listed preferences?",choices:["It offers every course on the list at once","It offers the highest-ranked course you qualify for","It ignores the order and chooses randomly","It always gives the lowest-ranked safe option first"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["UAC offers the highest-ranked course you qualify for.","","That is why preference order matters and why students should include both ambitious","and safer options."].join(`
`)},{id:"safe-preference",question:"Why is it sensible to include at least one safe option in your UAC preferences?",choices:["Because UAC requires the last preference to be a TAFE course","Because it gives you a realistic backup if your ATAR is lower than hoped","Because safe options always receive bonus points","Because universities reject students with ambitious preferences"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["It is sensible to include a safe option because it gives you a realistic backup if","your ATAR is lower than hoped.","","A balanced preference list reduces the chance of missing out on an offer entirely."].join(`
`)},{id:"offer-1-action",question:"After Main Round Offer 1 in early January, what may a student need to do?",choices:["Accept or decline the offer and decide whether to wait for another round","Sit the HSC exams again immediately","Submit Year 10 subject selections","Ask NESA to recalculate the ATAR manually"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["After Main Round Offer 1, a student may need to accept or decline the offer and then","decide whether to wait for another round.","","Understanding that process helps students move quickly once offers arrive."].join(`
`)},{id:"alternative-pathways",question:"If your ATAR is below the cutoff for your preferred course, which statement is most accurate?",choices:["There are no realistic options left","You must repeat Year 12 immediately","Alternative pathways such as later rounds, enabling programs, or transfer may still exist","Your only option is to change states"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["If your ATAR is below a cutoff, alternative pathways such as later rounds, enabling","programs, TAFE pathways, or internal transfer may still exist.","","Students should understand this before results day so they do not panic unnecessarily."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section VIII - University Entry, Adjustment Factors and Entry Schemes",description:"How prerequisites, assumed knowledge, bonus points, and entry schemes affect university entry beyond the raw ATAR.",questions:[{id:"assumed-knowledge",question:"If a university course lists assumed knowledge rather than a prerequisite, what does that mean?",choices:["You cannot apply without it","It is recommended, but not a compulsory entry requirement","It only matters for private universities","It replaces the ATAR completely"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Assumed knowledge is recommended, but it is not a compulsory entry requirement.","","A prerequisite is the hard requirement. Assumed knowledge means the university","expects that background will help you succeed once you start."].join(`
`)},{id:"adjustment-factors-meaning",question:"What do adjustment factors usually do?",choices:["They permanently change your official ATAR","They increase your selection rank for some university courses","They replace the HSC exam mark completely","They guarantee entry into any course you list"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Adjustment factors usually increase your selection rank for some university courses.","","They do not change your official ATAR itself. They only help in the university","selection process."].join(`
`)},{id:"official-atar-vs-selection-rank",question:"If your ATAR is 84.00 and a university gives you +5 adjustment points, which statement is correct?",choices:["Your official ATAR becomes 89.00 everywhere","Your official ATAR disappears and is replaced by 89.00","Your official ATAR stays 84.00, but your selection rank for that course may be treated as 89.00","You automatically receive an offer from every university"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Your official ATAR stays 84.00, but your selection rank for that course may be","treated as 89.00.","","That is the key idea: adjustment factors help with course selection, not with","changing the official ATAR itself."].join(`
`)},{id:"eas-purpose",question:"What is the Educational Access Scheme (EAS) mainly for?",choices:["Students who experienced disadvantage such as illness or disrupted schooling","Students who want to skip the UAC application process","Students who want to remove English from their ATAR pattern","Students who have already accepted a university offer"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["EAS is mainly for students who experienced disadvantage such as illness, financial","hardship, or disrupted schooling.","","It is one of the access schemes students should investigate early through UAC."].join(`
`)},{id:"srs-meaning",question:"What does SRS stand for in university entry?",choices:["Senior Results Scaling","Schools Recommendation Scheme","Student Ranking System","Special Review Selection"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["SRS stands for Schools Recommendation Scheme.","","It is an entry scheme that can help students receive offers based on school","performance information rather than relying only on the final ATAR."].join(`
`)},{id:"early-offer-meaning",question:"What is an early offer in university entry?",choices:["A university offer made before the main ATAR-based offer rounds through an earlier pathway","A guaranteed offer to every course in NSW","A rule that lets students ignore prerequisites","An automatic ATAR increase from UAC"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["An early offer is a university offer made before the main ATAR-based offer rounds","through an earlier pathway.","","It may be linked to schemes such as SRS or other special entry processes."].join(`
`)},{id:"early-offer-next-step",question:"If you receive an early offer, what is the best response?",choices:["Stop working for the HSC immediately","Ignore the offer because later offers always replace it","Read the conditions carefully and keep working toward your final results","Assume all your other preferences are cancelled automatically"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["If you receive an early offer, you should read the conditions carefully and keep","working toward your final results.","","Early offers can help reduce stress, but they do not always remove every later","requirement or make final HSC performance irrelevant."].join(`
`)},{id:"change-of-preference-window",question:"After ATAR release or an offer round, which statement about UAC preferences is correct?",choices:["Preferences are permanently locked for the rest of the year","Preferences can often be changed during short windows between rounds","Preferences can only be changed by your school","Preferences automatically reorder themselves after each offer round"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["UAC preferences can often be changed during short windows between offer rounds.","","That is why students should keep checking key dates after results and offers are","released."].join(`
`)},{id:"eas-documents",question:"What is one important reason students should start EAS applications early?",choices:["Because EAS automatically expires after one school day","Because supporting documents can take time to collect","Because EAS is only open before Year 11","Because EAS replaces the UAC application completely"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Students should start EAS applications early because supporting documents can take","time to collect and submit.","","Late or missing documents may mean the application is not considered in time for a","particular offer round."].join(`
`)},{id:"eas-no-guarantee",question:"If you are eligible for EAS, what should you still remember?",choices:["EAS guarantees an offer to your first preference","EAS guarantees your ATAR will be changed permanently","EAS can help, but it does not guarantee an offer","EAS means subject prerequisites no longer matter"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["EAS can help, but it does not guarantee an offer.","","You still need to compete for a place and meet the relevant course requirements."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section IX - Assessment and Admin Tasks",description:"Practical school and admin steps that students should stay on top of during Year 12.",questions:[{id:"assessment-schedule",question:"At the start of the HSC year, what is one of the most important documents to obtain and read carefully?",choices:["Your Year 6 report","Your school's Assessment Schedule","Your university enrolment form","Your school canteen timetable"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["One of the most important documents is your school's Assessment Schedule.","","It tells you when major tasks are due and helps you plan your workload across the","year instead of reacting too late."].join(`
`)},{id:"myhsc-purpose",question:"What is MyHSC mainly useful for?",choices:["Buying textbooks","Checking submitted coursework and marks information","Changing your ATAR after release","Replacing UAC applications"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["MyHSC is mainly useful for checking your submitted coursework and marks information.","","It is part of staying organised with the admin side of the HSC, not just the study","side."].join(`
`)},{id:"uac-deadlines-importance",question:"Why should students keep track of UAC deadlines early?",choices:["Because late applications can lead to extra fees or limited options","Because UAC deadlines decide your school rank","Because you must finalise preferences before Year 11","Because UAC deadlines replace HSC exams"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Students should track UAC deadlines early because late applications can mean extra","fees or limited course availability.","","This is an admin task that is easy to overlook if you only focus on exams."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section X - HSC Rules, Integrity and Exam Technique",description:"Exam-room rules, official systems, academic integrity, and the exam-technique habits that protect marks.",questions:[{id:"approved-calculator-rule",question:"Which calculator may be used in an HSC exam that allows calculators?",choices:["Any calculator sold at a school shop","Any scientific calculator as long as it looks basic","Only a NESA-approved calculator","A phone calculator in flight mode"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Only a NESA-approved calculator may be used in an HSC exam that allows calculators.","","Students should also know how to use it already and have it reset before entering the","exam room."].join(`
`)},{id:"exam-room-banned-item",question:"Which item is not permitted in the HSC exam room under any circumstance?",choices:["A clear unlabelled water bottle","A ruler","A mobile phone","A black pen"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["A mobile phone is not permitted in the exam room under any circumstance.","","NESA also bans smart watches and other unauthorised electronic devices."].join(`
`)},{id:"students-online-purpose",question:"What is Students Online mainly used for?",choices:["Buying textbooks and tutoring packages","Checking official details such as timetable, course entries, and results","Submitting university assignments","Replacing the UAC application system"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Students Online is used to check official details such as your timetable, course","entries, personal details, and results.","","It is an important official communication channel between students and NESA."].join(`
`)},{id:"minimum-standard-purpose",question:"What is the HSC minimum standard?",choices:["A bonus-point system for university entry","A separate literacy and numeracy requirement for receiving the HSC","The mark needed for a Band 6 in every subject","A ranking system used by UAC instead of the ATAR"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The HSC minimum standard is a separate literacy and numeracy requirement for","receiving the HSC.","","It is not the same thing as the ATAR."].join(`
`)},{id:"ai-academic-integrity",question:"Under NESA rules, unapproved use of AI in assessed work is best understood as:",choices:["Always acceptable if it improves the wording","A possible breach of academic integrity or malpractice rules","Something that only matters in university, not the HSC","Automatically allowed if the student does not copy exact sentences"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Unapproved use of AI in assessed work can be a breach of academic integrity or","malpractice rules.","","Students must follow school rules and ensure submitted work is their own or properly","acknowledged where allowed."].join(`
`)},{id:"practical-work-certification",question:"For a practical exam or submitted work, what must a student usually declare?",choices:["That the work was completed by a teacher","That the work is their own","That the work was generated by AI","That the work will automatically be selected for showcase"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["For a practical exam or submitted work, the student must usually declare that the","work is their own.","","Certification is part of how NESA maintains fairness in practical and major-project","style assessment."].join(`
`)},{id:"command-verb-explain",question:'If an HSC question says "Explain", what does that usually require?',choices:["Give only a short fact with no reasoning","Give reasons or causes that show how or why","Write a balanced essay with several competing viewpoints","Draw a diagram only"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:['If a question says "Explain", students usually need to give reasons or causes that',"show how or why.","","One of the easiest ways to lose marks is to describe something when the question","actually asks for explanation."].join(`
`)},{id:"time-by-marks",question:"What is the best exam-technique use of marks when planning time?",choices:["Write the same length answer for every question","Use the mark value as a guide to how much time and detail to give","Spend most of the paper on the first question only","Ignore the marks because markers only judge handwriting quality"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Students should use the mark value as a guide to how much time and detail to give.","","That helps stop over-writing low-mark questions and under-writing high-mark","responses."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section XI - Illness and Misadventure",description:"When the process applies, what it does not cover, and why timing and evidence matter.",questions:[{id:"illness-misadventure-purpose",question:"What is the main purpose of the HSC illness and misadventure process?",choices:["To automatically add bonus marks after any difficult exam","To consider serious disadvantage outside a student's control that affected performance","To replace all school assessment rules","To let students skip exams without explanation"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The process exists to consider serious disadvantage outside a student's control that","affected performance.","","It is a fairness process, not an automatic mark boost."].join(`
`)},{id:"illness-misadventure-not-covered",question:"Which situation is least likely to be covered by illness and misadventure?",choices:["A serious illness during the exam period","An unexpected family emergency","Poor preparation for the exam","A significant injury close to the exam"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Poor preparation is least likely to be covered by illness and misadventure.","","The process is for genuine serious disadvantage, not problems that were within the","student's control."].join(`
`)},{id:"illness-misadventure-timing",question:"If a serious problem affects you during an HSC exam, what is the best first response?",choices:["Wait a few weeks and decide later whether it mattered","Tell the presiding officer or exam supervisors as soon as possible","Post about it online and keep going without telling anyone","Assume the school will notice automatically"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["You should tell the presiding officer or exam supervisors as soon as possible.","","Prompt reporting helps establish what happened and supports any later application."].join(`
`)},{id:"illness-misadventure-evidence",question:"What usually makes an illness and misadventure application stronger?",choices:["A vague explanation with no supporting documents","Independent evidence such as a medical certificate or professional statement","A message from a friend saying the exam felt hard","A promise to study more next time"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Independent evidence such as a medical certificate or professional statement usually","makes the application stronger.","","Evidence should be prompt, specific, and relevant to the timing of the problem."].join(`
`)},{id:"illness-misadventure-guarantee",question:"Which statement about illness and misadventure is most accurate?",choices:["It guarantees the mark a student hoped to receive","It is a fairness process, but it does not guarantee a particular outcome","It only applies to university offers, not the HSC","It is mainly for students who forgot to bring equipment"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Illness and misadventure is a fairness process, but it does not guarantee a particular","outcome.","","Students still need to follow the correct process and provide proper evidence."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section XII - Pattern Checking, Course Types and Year-Level Rules",description:"Scenario-based checks for ATAR patterns, course types, VET, and the difference between Year 11, Year 12, and ATAR rules.",questions:[{id:"hsc-vs-atar-distinction",question:"Which statement best describes the difference between the HSC and the ATAR?",choices:["They are exactly the same thing with different names","The HSC is the credential from NESA; the ATAR is the university-rank calculation from UAC","The ATAR is issued by schools, while the HSC is issued by universities","The HSC only matters in Year 11, while the ATAR replaces it in Year 12"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The HSC is the credential from NESA, while the ATAR is the rank calculation from UAC.","","Students often confuse these because they arrive at the end of Year 12, but they are","different parts of the system."].join(`
`)},{id:"science-heavy-pattern-valid",question:"Which pattern is most likely to be valid for ATAR subject-area purposes?",choices:["English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Chemistry, Physics","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Mathematics Extension 2, Biology","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Mathematics Extension 2, Physics","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Biology, Science Extension"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Chemistry, and Physics is most likely","to be valid because it reaches 10 units and gives enough distinct subject areas.","","A science-heavy pattern can still be valid if it reaches the required breadth."].join(`
`)},{id:"short-list-not-full-pattern",question:'A guide shows "English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Modern History" to illustrate subject areas. Why is that not automatically a full ATAR-valid pattern?',choices:["Because it has only 8 units, even though it shows 4 subject areas","Because Modern History never counts toward the ATAR","Because Biology and Modern History are the same subject area","Because English Advanced cannot be combined with Mathematics Advanced"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["That short list shows 4 subject areas, but on its own it has only 8 units, so it is","not automatically a full ATAR-valid pattern.","","Students need to check unit count as well as subject-area breadth."].join(`
`)},{id:"pattern-failure-reason-diagnostic",question:"Which option fails mainly because it is below the 10-unit ATAR minimum, not because of subject-area concentration?",choices:["English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Modern History","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Mathematics Extension 2, Biology","English Advanced, English Extension 1, English Extension 2, Mathematics Advanced, Mathematics Extension 1, Biology, Science Extension","English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, Modern History, Studies of Religion II"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["English Advanced, Mathematics Advanced, Biology, and Modern History fails mainly","because it is only 8 units on its own.","","The other incorrect options are more about subject-area concentration, while the last","option is a full 10-unit valid pattern."].join(`
`)},{id:"extension-heavy-pattern-fail",question:"Why can an extension-heavy pattern still fail ATAR eligibility?",choices:["Because extension courses are never allowed in Year 12","Because it may still have only 3 subject areas even with many units","Because English Extension removes the need for English Standard or Advanced","Because UAC ignores every course worth 1 unit"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["An extension-heavy pattern can still fail if it has only 3 subject areas, even with","many units.","","That is why students must count subject areas, not just courses or units."].join(`
`)},{id:"board-endorsed-vs-vet-scenario",question:'Which statement best handles a student saying, "My Board Endorsed course and my VET course will both automatically count toward my ATAR"?',choices:["That is always true for every Board Endorsed and VET course","Board Endorsed courses generally do not count, and a VET course counts only if it is listed as an ATAR course and the optional exam is sat","Only Board Endorsed courses count automatically, not VET courses","Only VET courses count automatically, not Board Endorsed courses"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Board Endorsed courses generally do not count toward the ATAR, and a VET course only","counts if it is listed as an ATAR course and the student sits the optional HSC exam.","","Students should never assume that timetable presence automatically means ATAR use."].join(`
`)},{id:"board-endorsed-atar-rule",question:"Which statement is most accurate about Board Endorsed courses?",choices:["They are usually part of HSC study but generally not used in ATAR calculations","They always count toward the ATAR automatically","They are the same thing as Board Developed courses","They replace all Board Developed courses in Year 12"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Board Endorsed courses are usually part of HSC study, but they are generally not used","in ATAR calculations.","","Students should not assume every course in an HSC timetable automatically helps with","the ATAR pattern."].join(`
`)},{id:"vet-optional-exam-rule",question:"When can a VET course contribute to the ATAR?",choices:["Whenever a school offers the course, even without an HSC exam","Only if it is listed as an ATAR course and the student sits the optional HSC exam","Only if the student studies two VET courses together","Only in Year 11, not Year 12"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["A VET course can contribute to the ATAR only if it is listed as an ATAR course and","the student sits the optional HSC exam.","","That is why students should always check the current UAC HSC course list."].join(`
`)},{id:"year11-vs-year12-patterns",question:"Which statement about Year 11 and Year 12 is most accurate?",choices:["Year 11 Preliminary results directly generate the ATAR","Year 11 builds the foundation, while the Year 12 HSC course is what counts directly toward the ATAR","Year 12 only matters for internal assessment, not for UAC","Year 11 and Year 12 follow exactly the same study-pattern rules in every detail"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Year 11 builds the foundation, while the Year 12 HSC course is what counts directly","toward the ATAR.","","Students should also remember that HSC completion rules and ATAR eligibility rules","are related but not identical."].join(`
`)},{id:"year11-vs-year12-minimums",question:"Which pair of minimums best matches the standard NESA study pattern described in the guides?",choices:["Year 11: 10 units, Year 12: 8 units","Year 11: 12 units, Year 12: 10 units","Year 11: 12 units, Year 12: 12 units","Year 11: 10 units, Year 12: 10 units"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The guides describe the standard NESA pattern as at least 12 units in Year 11 and","at least 10 units in Year 12.","","That is separate from the extra UAC ATAR requirements layered on top."].join(`
`)}]},{title:"Section XIII - Prerequisites and Course Chains",description:"Checks whether students can distinguish HSC course prerequisites from ATAR eligibility and university-entry rules.",questions:[{id:"prerequisite-vs-atar-rule",question:"What is the most accurate difference between an HSC course prerequisite and an ATAR rule?",choices:["They are the same rule expressed in different words","A prerequisite is part of the course chain, while an ATAR rule is part of UAC eligibility","ATAR rules matter only in Year 11, while prerequisites matter only after school","A prerequisite is optional if the student is aiming for a high ATAR"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["A course prerequisite is part of the course chain, while an ATAR rule belongs to UAC","eligibility calculations.","","Students get into trouble when they treat course-structure rules and ATAR pattern","rules as if they were interchangeable."].join(`
